#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse a commit in the primary checkout while another session is live in it.
#
# Installed by run.sh, which points core.hooksPath here. Not active in a
# worktree: a worktree has its own working tree, so its commits can only hold
# its own edits, which is the whole point of taking one.
#
# The incident: two sessions worked in the shared checkout at once, and a
# `git add -A` in one of them swept up the other's unstaged edits - a paragraph
# in docs/members.md and a deleted row in docs/questions.csv - into a commit
# whose message described neither. Nothing was lost, but the history now
# describes work it does not mention, and the next reader of that commit is
# owed an explanation nobody can give.
#
# Staging explicit paths avoids it, but only while everyone remembers to. This
# makes the shared checkout refuse instead, so the fix is structural: take a
# worktree and the edits cannot mix.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

[ -f .claude/sessions.sh ] || exit 0

live=$(bash .claude/sessions.sh others | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
[ "$live" -eq 0 ] && exit 0

cat >&2 <<EOF

Refusing this commit: $live other Claude sessions are live in this checkout.

A commit here would carry whatever anyone else has in flight, under your
message. That has happened (docs/repository.md).

Work on a branch in a worktree instead, where nobody else is editing:

    git worktree add .claude/worktrees/<name> -b <branch>

Then commit there. To commit here anyway, knowing the diff may hold another
session's edits:

    git commit --no-verify

EOF
exit 1
